<h1>Writing a bootloader to load Linux</h2>
<h3>NOTE: this is not a guide as I have no idea what I am doing</h3>
<p>Writing a bootloader was one of the things I always wanted to do. So I did it, for my weekend project; it was hard. I will assume you know assembly before we begin.</p>
<h2>Some background</h2>
<p>When the computer loads up it starts the BIOS (or the UEFI firmware) which performs certain hardware tests called POST. During POST the BIOS also finds the Master Boot Record (MBR), this MBR is what I have written, and fit the entire bootloader in it. <br> The MBR is a 512 byte file (which is tiny!) that contains instructions on how to boot into the operating system. It must end with 0x55AA.</p>
<h2>Hello World</h2>
<p>To do a hello world we call BIOS interrupts to do stuff</p>
<pre><code>[BITS 16]                       ; tell nasm to compile this part as 16 bit real mode machine code
[ORG 0x7c00]                    ; tell nasm that this file will load at 0x7c00

start:
    mov si, msg

print: 
    lodsb                       ; load byte by byte from data segment (ds) into al using si (ds:si)
    cmp al, 0                   ; check if character is a null byte
    je done
    mov ah, 0x0e                ; 0x0e = write character in tty
    int 0x10                    ; video interrupt
    jmp print

done:   
    hlt
 msg:
     db "Hello World", 0

;; pad the rest of the file
times 510-($-$$) db 0
dw 0xAA55
</code></pre>
<p>If you have written assembly Linux assembly with syscalls, you probably understand most of it. Compile and run using:</p>
<pre><code>nasm -f bin boot.asm -o boot.bin
qemu-system-i386 -m 512 -drive format=raw,file=./boot.bin
</code></pre>
<p>You should see something like this: (I ran qemu with -nographic flag)</p>
<pre><code>SeaBIOS (version 1.17.0-10.fc44)


iPXE (https://ipxe.org) 00:03.0 CA00 PCI2.10 PnP PMM+1EFCBE40+1EF0BE40 CA00
                                                                               


Booting from Hard Disk...
Hello World
</code></pre>
<h2>Linux Boot Protocol</h2>
<p>How you write the bootloader depends on the kernel you are trying to load. The Linux kernel has a fixed boot protocol which you can find <a href="https://www.kernel.org/doc/html/v6.1/x86/boot.html">here</a></p>
